# Cellular Automata as Polynomial Worlds

A line of binary cells can be read in two ways: as a dynamical system evolving through time, or as a point acted on by polynomial equations over $\mathbb F_2$. Begin with the local bridge.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The Rule 110 lookup table and the polynomial $r+c+cr+\ell cr$ agree on all eight neighborhoods. The following visualization displays that complete truth table, while the algorithm reveals how any of the $256$ tables can be transformed into a unique multilinear polynomial.

{{visualization:0}}

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary><strong>Reveal the algebraic-normal-form argument</strong></summary>
The eight square-free monomials indexed by subsets of three variables form a basis for all functions on $\mathbb F_2^3$. Their evaluation matrix is triangular with unit diagonal when subsets are ordered by inclusion. The Boolean Möbius transform computes the coordinates in this basis in $O(k2^k)$ time for $k$ inputs.
</details>

## Fixed configurations on a periodic ring

A fixed state satisfies $F_R(s)=s$ at every cell. Use the ring workbench to create examples and see whether the next state equals the current state.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

Rule $0$ has exactly one fixed state at every positive ring length; Rule $204$ fixes all $2^n$ states. Rule $110$ fixes the zero state but excludes the one state, so its fixed locus is nonempty and proper.

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary><strong>Reveal the extremal proofs</strong></summary>
For Rule $0$, every local output is zero, so equality with the old state forces every cell to be zero. Rule $204$ outputs its center input, so every cell reproduces itself. For Rule $110$, substitution into $r+c+cr+\ell cr$ gives $0$ at both $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1)$; hence all zeros remain fixed, while all ones become all zeros.
</details>

## Why computation is not stillness

The maximal fixed locus belongs to the identity Rule $204$, not to universal Rule $110$. Computational richness involves moving signals and long histories. Compare the three systems directly.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary><strong>Dimensions that must not be conflated</strong></summary>
For finite Boolean rings, fixed-point count, Krull dimension, coordinate-algebra vector-space dimension, and asymptotic fixed-point entropy are different invariants. In particular, the Boolean coordinate algebra is zero-dimensional even when there are $2^n$ rational points. A promising replacement studies spacetime variables $x_{i,t}$ and equations $x_{i,t+1}=p_R(x_{i-1,t},x_{i,t},x_{i+1,t})$.
</details>

## Continue exploring

Run the truth-table checker and spacetime renderer, then vary the rule and period. Useful background includes [cellular automata](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cellular_automaton), [finite fields](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finite_field), [algebraic normal form](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algebraic_normal_form), and [symbolic dynamics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbolic_dynamics).

